---
Part IX, Chapter 8: Testing the grammar
Package: packages/tree-sitter-loom/test/conformance.test.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# Testing the grammar

The grammar is a second reading of a loom, and a second reading is only useful while it agrees with the first. Loom's own classifier decides the kind of every line, and every later pass builds on that decision. So the test of the grammar is agreement: for every line of a loom, the grammar must place the line in the block the classifier gives it. The test reads every loom in the repository both ways and compares them line by line, then does the same for a few looms built to reach the corners the repository does not.

Two smaller checks come first. The parser in `src/` must be the one `grammar.js` generates today, since editors compile the committed parser and never run the generator. And a loom must parse without an error node, since an editor colours an error node as broken text.

## Two readings of a line

A line's kind, for this comparison, is the block the line belongs to, or the mark it opens with. The frontmatter, a heading, and a divider are kinds of their own. A code block starts at its arrow and continues as code, and a prose block starts at its tilde. A fence around fenced code is a kind of its own, and so is a line that opens or closes a box. Every other line of prose is text, a box's body and a stray line of colons included. The classifier tells a preamble line from a prose line by the mode it is in, and the grammar folds both into its prose blocks, so the comparison names both text.

`kindOfWeft` maps each weft the classifier emits to its kind. It is a record over every weft's type, so a new weft kind fails to compile here until the test decides what it is. A divider is a line of its own kind, as it is to the grammar. A box line is a preamble weft or a prose weft by its type, so `kindOf` first asks `boxOf` whether the weft carries a box that opens or closes, and asks the record only when it does not.

=>

type LineKind =
  | 'frontmatter'
  | 'heading'
  | 'divider'
  | 'arrow'
  | 'code'
  | 'fence'
  | 'tilde'
  | 'box'
  | 'text'
  | 'none'

const kindOfWeft: Record<LoomWeft['type'], LineKind> = {
  FrontmatterWeft: 'frontmatter',
  HeadingWeft: 'heading',
  ArrowWeft: 'arrow',
  CodeWeft: 'code',
  FenceWeft: 'fence',
  TildeWeft: 'tilde',
  PreambleWeft: 'text',
  ProseWeft: 'text',
  DividerWeft: 'divider',
}

const kindOf = (weft: LoomWeft): LineKind =>
  pipe(
    boxOf(weft),
    Option.filter((box) => box.side !== 'stray'),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => kindOfWeft[weft.type],
      onSome: (): LineKind => 'box',
    }),
  )

## The classifier's reading

The classifier reads a loom as Part III describes: `LoomSourceRanges` cuts the text into lines, and `WeftClassifier` emits a weft for each. `classifierKinds` runs the two stages and gives every weft its kind.

=>

const classifierKinds = (text: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<LineKind>> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const sources = yield* LoomSourceRanges
    const classifier = yield* WeftClassifier
    const ranges = yield* sources.stream(text)
    const wefts = yield* Stream.runCollect(classifier.classifyWefts(text)(ranges))
    return Array.map(wefts, kindOf)
  }).pipe(
    Effect.provide(LoomSourceRanges.layer),
    Effect.provide(WeftClassifier.layer),
    Effect.orDie,
  )

~

The two readings count lines differently at the end of a file. A text that ends with a line break leaves an empty range after it, which the classifier reads as a last empty line and the grammar reads as no line at all. `lineCount` counts the lines the grammar sees, and the comparison takes that many from the classifier.

=>

const lineBreak = /\r\n|\r|\n/

const lineCount = (text: string): number =>
  text.length === 0 ? 0 : text.split(lineBreak).length - (/(\r\n|\r|\n)$/.test(text) ? 1 : 0)

## The grammar's reading

The test drives the `tree-sitter` command from the `tree-sitter-cli` package, the same version that generated the parser. `treeSitter` runs it in the grammar's folder and returns what it printed.

=>

const grammarDir = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..')

const treeSitterBinary = join(
  dirname(createRequire(import.meta.url).resolve('tree-sitter-cli/package.json')),
  process.platform === 'win32' ? 'tree-sitter.exe' : 'tree-sitter',
)

const treeSitter = (args: ReadonlyArray<string>): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  Effect.sync(
    () =>
      spawnSync(treeSitterBinary, args, {
        cwd: grammarDir,
        encoding: 'utf8',
        maxBuffer: 1 << 28,
      }).stdout,
  )

~

The parser is compiled once, into a scratch folder, before any test runs, and every loom is parsed with that one library. The command would otherwise keep its own compiled copy in a shared cache and decide for itself when to rebuild it.

Compiling the parser takes a C compiler a few seconds on a fast machine, and longer on a cold CI runner. Bun gives a hook five seconds unless it is told otherwise, and from Bun 1.4 on it holds `beforeAll` to that limit too, failing the whole file when the compile runs past it. So the hook allows two minutes, `compileTime`.

=>

const scratch = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'tree-sitter-loom-'))
const library = join(scratch, 'loom.so')

const compileTime = 120_000

beforeAll(() => Effect.runPromise(treeSitter(['build', '--output', library])), { timeout: compileTime })

const parseTree = (file: string): Effect.Effect<string> =>
  treeSitter(['parse', '--lib-path', library, '--lang-name', 'loom', file])

~

The command prints the tree as nested nodes, each with the row and column where it starts and ends. `grammarKinds` reads the six block nodes off that print and gives each row the kind its block assigns: code in a code block, text in the rest of prose. A block ends at the start of the row after its last line, at column zero, so its rows run up to that row. A block can hold another, as fenced code holds the prose its closing fence hands on, and the print lists the inner block after the outer, so its rows win.

The marks then name the rows they open. An arrow, a tilde, a code fence, and the colons of a box each mark the row they stand on, and `markRows` gives that row the mark's own kind over its block's. A row that no block claims has no kind, and the comparison then reports it.

=>

type Block = 'frontmatter' | 'heading' | 'divider' | 'preamble' | 'code' | 'prose'

const blockKind: Record<Block, LineKind> = {
  frontmatter: 'frontmatter',
  heading: 'heading',
  divider: 'divider',
  preamble: 'text',
  code: 'code',
  prose: 'text',
}

const blockPattern =
  /\((frontmatter|heading|divider|preamble|code|prose) \[(\d+), \d+\] - \[(\d+), (\d+)\]/g

const rowsOf = (match: RegExpMatchArray): ReadonlyArray<readonly [number, LineKind]> => {
  const [, block, startRow, endRow, endColumn] = match
  const start = Number(startRow)
  const end = Number(endColumn) === 0 ? Number(endRow) - 1 : Number(endRow)
  return Array.map(Array.range(start, end), (row) => [row, blockKind[block as Block]] as const)
}

type Mark = 'arrow' | 'tilde' | 'code_fence' | 'box_fence'

const markKind: Record<Mark, LineKind> = {
  arrow: 'arrow',
  tilde: 'tilde',
  code_fence: 'fence',
  box_fence: 'box',
}

const markPattern = /\((arrow|tilde|code_fence|box_fence) \[(\d+), \d+\]/g

const markRows = (tree: string): ReadonlyArray<readonly [number, LineKind]> =>
  Array.map(
    Array.fromIterable(tree.matchAll(markPattern)),
    ([, mark, row]) => [Number(row), markKind[mark as Mark]] as const,
  )

const grammarKinds = (tree: string, count: number): ReadonlyArray<LineKind> => {
  const rows = new Map([
    ...Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(tree.matchAll(blockPattern)), rowsOf),
    ...markRows(tree),
  ])
  return count === 0 ? [] : Array.map(Array.range(0, count - 1), (row) => rows.get(row) ?? 'none')
}

## Where the readings part

`disagreements` lines the two readings up and keeps every line where they differ, with its number and both kinds. An empty list is agreement, and a failing test prints the lines that disagree rather than two long lists.

=>

type Disagreement = {
  readonly line: number
  readonly classifier: LineKind
  readonly grammar: LineKind
}

const disagreements = (
  classifier: ReadonlyArray<LineKind>,
  grammar: ReadonlyArray<LineKind>,
): ReadonlyArray<Disagreement> =>
  pipe(
    Array.zip(classifier, grammar),
    Array.map(([c, g], i) => ({ line: i + 1, classifier: c, grammar: g })),
    Array.filter((d) => d.classifier !== d.grammar),
  )

~

`checkLoom` puts a file through both readings. The grammar's tree must hold no error and no missing node, and its kinds must agree with the classifier's on every line.

=>

const checkLoom = (file: string): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const text = readFileSync(file, 'utf8')
    const tree = yield* parseTree(file)
    expect(tree).not.toMatch(/\((ERROR|MISSING)/)
    const count = lineCount(text)
    const classifier = Array.take(yield* classifierKinds(text), count)
    expect(disagreements(classifier, grammarKinds(tree, count))).toEqual([])
  })

## The parser is generated, and current

The test regenerates the parser from `grammar.js` into the scratch folder and compares it with the committed `src/parser.c`. A difference means someone changed the grammar and did not run `bun run generate`, and every editor would still compile the old parser.

=>

describe('the generated parser', () => {
  it.effect(
    'matches what grammar.js generates',
    () =>
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        yield* treeSitter(['generate', '--output', join(scratch, 'generated')])
        expect(readFileSync(join(scratch, 'generated', 'parser.c'), 'utf8')).toBe(
          readFileSync(join(grammarDir, 'src', 'parser.c'), 'utf8'),
        )
      }),
    60_000,
  )
})

## Every loom in the repository

The repository's own looms are the widest sample there is: the book, the docs, and the examples, about a hundred files in every form a loom takes. `repositoryLooms` lists them, leaving out installed packages and hidden folders such as a project's `.loom` store. Each loom is a test of its own, so a failure names its file.

=>

const repository = resolve(grammarDir, '..', '..')

const repositoryLooms: ReadonlyArray<string> = pipe(
  ['corpus', 'docs', 'examples'],
  Array.flatMap((folder) =>
    Array.map(
      readdirSync(join(repository, folder), { recursive: true, encoding: 'utf8' }),
      (path) => join(folder, path),
    ),
  ),
  Array.filter(
    (path) =>
      path.endsWith('.loom') &&
      path.split(/[\\/]/).every((part) => part !== 'node_modules' && !part.startsWith('.')),
  ),
)

describe('the grammar agrees with the classifier', () => {
  Array.forEach(repositoryLooms, (path) =>
    it.effect(path, () => checkLoom(join(repository, path)), 30_000),
  )
})

## The corners the book does not reach

A few forms are rare in the repository, or absent from it: fenced code that holds `#` lines and a shorter fence, Windows line endings, a last line with no line break, a tilde that follows a fenced block, braces that touch an anchor outside a code span, boxes of every shape a box line takes, and a discussion whose identity line joins its part. Each case here is a small loom of its own. As in the CLI's tests, a fixture is a list of quoted lines, so no line of it is read as part of this loom, and `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces. `fixture` writes the lines with a chosen line ending and returns the file.

=>

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const fixture = (name: string, rows: ReadonlyArray<string>, ending: string = '\n'): string => {
  const file = join(scratch, `${name}.loom`)
  writeFileSync(file, rows.map((row) => `${row}${ending}`).join(''))
  return file
}

~

Fenced code keeps a Markdown file's headings and a script's comments as code, and only a fence that matches its own ends it. This one opens with four backticks, so the three inside it stay code. The prose after the closing fence needs no tilde. A plain block ends at the next heading.

=>

const fencedBlock = [
  '---',
  'Language: Markdown',
  '---',
  '',
  '# A README [README.md]',
  '',
  '=>',
  '````md',
  '# Title',
  '',
  '=> not an arrow here',
  '~ nor a tilde',
  '```sh',
  '## Usage',
  '````',
  '',
  'Back in prose.',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  anchor('Usage'),
  '# The next section',
]

~

A fence in a section's preamble switches the classifier into prose mode, so a tilde after the fence is a line of prose rather than a transition. No mark in a box's body opens anything. A heading, an arrow, a fence, and three colons with a name all stay the body's text, and only three colons alone close the box.

=>

const fenceAndBox = [
  '# Quoting a loom',
  '',
  '```loom',
  '# Not a heading',
  '=>',
  '```',
  '~ still prose',
  '',
  '::: A remark {Note}',
  '# Also not a heading',
  '=> nor an arrow',
  '```',
  '::: nor a box inside the box',
  ':::',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  'code()',
]

~

Braces that touch an anchor belong to it, in prose and in code. The prose holds an anchor whose kind is a label of three words. The code holds one anchor with two parameters, a quoted value and a list, and one with a target and a parameter. An anchor followed by a space and a brace leaves the brace to the code. The tree reads three sets of braces and three parameters, and no error.

=>

const anchorBraces = [
  '# Greeting',
  '',
  `A greeting waits for ${anchor('Greeting function')}{To be settled}.`,
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  `${anchor('Greeting function')}{kind="To be settled" provides=[greet, farewell]}`,
  `${anchor('Saying hello')}(room.ts){provides=greet}`,
  `const origin = ${anchor('Point')} {X: 1}`,
]

~

A box line holds a name, braces, or both, and braces belong to the box only where they end the line. The fixture `boxLines` holds a note with an anchor in its body, a chapter box with a parameter, an indented note with no name, a name with braces inside it, a stray of four colons, and a box that never closes, whose body runs to the end of the file. The tree reads four names, two sets of braces, one parameter, and the one anchor, and no error.

=>

const boxLines = [
  '# Boxes',
  '',
  '::: Why a grid {Note}',
  `The room is small, as ${anchor('Room')} says.`,
  ':::',
  '',
  '::: Rooms {kind=Chapter}',
  ':::',
  '',
  '  :::',
  '  A note with no name.',
  '  :::',
  '',
  '::: Why {x} matters',
  ':::',
  '',
  ':::: Four colons open nothing',
  '',
  '::: Never closed',
  '# Still the body',
]

~

A discussion reads its lines by rules of its own, as the chapter "Discussions" describes. A tilde opens a message wherever it stands, even straight after a line of prose, and an arrow opens nothing, so its line is prose. Its identity line may join its part, and the scanner reads it all the same. So the parse holds a tilde for each of the two messages and no arrow.

=>

const discussion = [
  '---',
  'Part I, Discussion 1: Waking',
  '---',
  '',
  '# Waking',
  '',
  'Whether a monster wakes on its own.',
  '',
  'A monster that sleeps',
  '---',
  '',
  '~ Yegor',
  'It should wait for the player.',
  '=> and this arrow is only words',
  '~ Claude',
  'Then no timer starts on its own.',
  '',
  '::: {Conclusion}',
  'A monster waits for the player.',
  ':::',
]

describe('the corners', () => {
  it.effect('fenced code holds headings, arrows and shorter fences as code', () =>
    checkLoom(fixture('fenced', fencedBlock)),
  )
  it.effect('Windows line endings read as Unix ones do', () =>
    checkLoom(fixture('crlf', fencedBlock, '\r\n')),
  )
  it.effect('a fence and a box keep their marks inert', () =>
    checkLoom(fixture('fence-and-box', fenceAndBox)),
  )
  it.effect('a box line holds its name and its braces', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const file = fixture('box-lines', boxLines)
      yield* checkLoom(file)
      const tree = yield* parseTree(file)
      expect(tree.match(/\(box_name /g)).toHaveLength(4)
      expect(tree.match(/\(box_specifier /g)).toHaveLength(2)
      expect(tree.match(/\(parameter /g)).toHaveLength(1)
      expect(tree.match(/\(anchor /g)).toHaveLength(1)
    }),
  )
  it.effect('braces that touch an anchor hold its label or its parameters', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const file = fixture('anchor-braces', anchorBraces)
      yield* checkLoom(file)
      const tree = yield* parseTree(file)
      expect(tree.match(/\(anchor_specifier /g)).toHaveLength(3)
      expect(tree.match(/\(parameter /g)).toHaveLength(3)
    }),
  )
  it.effect('a discussion reads a tilde as a message and an arrow as prose', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const file = fixture('discussion', discussion)
      yield* checkLoom(file)
      const tree = yield* parseTree(file)
      expect(tree.match(/\(tilde /g)).toHaveLength(2)
      expect(tree.match(/\(arrow /g)).toBeNull()
    }),
  )
  it.effect('a last line needs no line break', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const file = join(scratch, 'unterminated.loom')
      writeFileSync(file, fenceAndBox.join('\n'))
      yield* checkLoom(file)
    }),
  )
})

## Each code block takes one language

The injection queries decide the language each code block is coloured in. They come in three copies: the shared one of Chapter 6, which Neovim reads, Helix's beside it, and Zed's in Chapter 10. A code block that no pattern matches reads as plain text. A code block that two patterns match leaves the editor to choose between them. So the test runs each copy over one loom, which has a section for each way a section names its language. For every code block, it asks that exactly one pattern injects the block, with the language that copy's editor should read. It also asks that the injected text leaves out the block's anchors, which belong to Loom and not to the section's language.

The `tree-sitter query` command prints each match as its pattern's number followed by its captures. Each capture shows the row and column where it starts and ends and, when the capture fits on one line, its text. The `injectionOf` function reads one match into an injection. An injection holds the spans of its content, the row its first span starts on, and the text of the capture that names its language, by a name or by a file. A span is a start and an end, each a row and a column, and the end lies just past the span's last character. A pattern that sets its language with `#set!` writes the language in the query rather than in the loom, so the test records that injection's language as `fixedLanguage`, the text `#set!`. The `injectionsOf` function runs the command and reads every match it prints.

=>

type Position = readonly [row: number, column: number]
type Span = { readonly start: Position; readonly end: Position }
type Injection = {
  readonly row: number
  readonly language: string
  readonly content: ReadonlyArray<Span>
}

const fixedLanguage = '#set!'

const capturePattern =
  /capture: (?:\d+ - )?([\w.]+), start: \((\d+), (\d+)\), end: \((\d+), (\d+)\)(?:, text: `([^`\n]*)`)?$/gm

const injectionOf = (match: string): ReadonlyArray<Injection> => {
  const captures = Array.fromIterable(match.matchAll(capturePattern))
  const named = Array.findFirst(
    captures,
    ([, name]) => name === 'injection.language' || name === 'injection.filename',
  )
  const content = pipe(
    captures,
    Array.filter(([, name]) => name === 'injection.content'),
    Array.map(([, , startRow, startColumn, endRow, endColumn]) => ({
      start: [Number(startRow), Number(startColumn)] as const,
      end: [Number(endRow), Number(endColumn)] as const,
    })),
  )
  return pipe(
    Array.head(content),
    Option.map((first) => ({
      row: first.start[0],
      language: Option.match(named, {
        onNone: () => fixedLanguage,
        onSome: ([, , , , , , text]) => text ?? '',
      }),
      content,
    })),
    Option.toArray,
  )
}

const injectionsOf = (query: string, file: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Injection>> =>
  Effect.map(
    treeSitter(['query', '--lib-path', library, '--lang-name', 'loom', query, file]),
    (printed) => Array.flatMap(printed.split(/^\s*pattern: \d+$/m).slice(1), injectionOf),
  )

~

The loom, `languageForms`, is a chapter whose frontmatter names its file `src/out.ts` and its language TypeScript. Its five sections name their languages five ways: by a specifier, by a sink, by `{Tangle}` alone, by nothing at all, and by `{Prose}`. Four anchors sit where they could split a block. The `{Tangle}` section draws one in on its arrow's own line, and the section that names nothing draws one in between two lines of its own code and two more side by side.

The `codeLanguages` function finds each code block by its arrow. The block runs from the arrow to the next heading, and an injection belongs to the block where its first span starts. The function lists the language every injection gives each block. A block injected twice shows two languages, even when the second injection starts after an anchor, and a block never injected shows none.

=>

const languageForms = [
  '---',
  'Package: src/out.ts',
  'Language: TypeScript',
  '---',
  '',
  '# A specifier {json}',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  '{}',
  '',
  '# A sink [run.sh]',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  'echo',
  '',
  '# The Package file {Tangle}',
  '',
  `=> ${anchor('A sink')}`,
  '',
  'export {}',
  '',
  '# The chapter',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  'const a = 1',
  anchor('A specifier'),
  `${anchor('A sink')}${anchor('A specifier')}`,
  'export { a }',
  '',
  '# A Prose section {Prose}',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  'Some text.',
]

const rowsWhere = (test: (line: string) => boolean): ReadonlyArray<number> =>
  Array.filter(Array.range(0, languageForms.length - 1), (row) => test(languageForms[row]))

const headingRows = rowsWhere((line) => line.startsWith('# '))

const blockEnd = (arrow: number): number =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Array.findFirst(headingRows, (row) => row > arrow),
    () => languageForms.length,
  )

const codeLanguages = (query: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>>> =>
  Effect.map(injectionsOf(query, fixture('language-forms', languageForms)), (injections) =>
    Array.map(
      rowsWhere((line) => line.startsWith('=>')),
      (arrow) =>
        pipe(
          injections,
          Array.filter((injection) => injection.row >= arrow && injection.row < blockEnd(arrow)),
          Array.map((injection) => injection.language),
        ),
    ),
  )

~

Each copy gives the names its editor reads. The shared copy and Helix's pass a file by its whole name, `run.sh` or the `Package` file `src/out.ts`. Helix knows TypeScript only by its lower-case name, so its copy sets that name with `#set!`. Zed's copy passes a sink's extension, `sh`, and gives the `{Tangle}` section the chapter's `Language`.

=>

describe('the injections', () => {
  it.effect('the shared queries inject each code block once', () =>
    Effect.map(codeLanguages('queries/injections.scm'), (languages) =>
      expect(languages).toEqual([
        ['json'],
        ['run.sh'],
        ['src/out.ts'],
        ['TypeScript'],
        [fixedLanguage],
      ]),
    ),
  )
  it.effect("Helix's queries inject each code block once", () =>
    Effect.map(codeLanguages('editors/helix/queries/loom/injections.scm'), (languages) =>
      expect(languages).toEqual([
        ['json'],
        ['run.sh'],
        ['src/out.ts'],
        [fixedLanguage],
        [fixedLanguage],
      ]),
    ),
  )
  it.effect("Zed's queries inject each code block once", () =>
    Effect.map(codeLanguages('editors/zed/languages/loom/injections.scm'), (languages) =>
      expect(languages).toEqual([
        ['json'],
        ['sh'],
        ['TypeScript'],
        ['TypeScript'],
        [fixedLanguage],
      ]),
    ),
  )
})

~

An anchor stays out of the injected text when no span of any injection covers its first character. `covers` compares positions row first, then column, and holds when a span starts at or before the position and ends after it. Each copy is checked, because each copy writes its own patterns.

=>

const anchorRow = languageForms.indexOf(anchor('A specifier'))

const before = (a: Position, b: Position): boolean => a[0] < b[0] || (a[0] === b[0] && a[1] <= b[1])

const covers = (span: Span, position: Position): boolean =>
  before(span.start, position) && !before(span.end, position)

describe('the anchors in a code block', () => {
  Array.forEach(
    [
      'queries/injections.scm',
      'editors/helix/queries/loom/injections.scm',
      'editors/zed/languages/loom/injections.scm',
    ],
    (query) =>
      it.effect(`stay out of the text ${query} injects`, () =>
        Effect.map(injectionsOf(query, fixture('language-forms', languageForms)), (injections) =>
          expect(
            Array.some(injections, (injection) =>
              Array.some(injection.content, (span) => covers(span, [anchorRow, 0])),
            ),
          ).toBe(false),
        ),
      ),
  )
})

## The pins name the grammar's last commit

Zed and Helix compile the grammar at the commit their manifests pin, and Chapter 11 sets the rule: the pin names the last commit that changed `src/`. The test holds every push to that rule. It asks Git for the commit, reads the revision out of Zed's `extension.toml` and Helix's `languages.toml`, and expects both to name it. A commit that changes the grammar fails this test until the next commit moves the pin, which `bun scripts/pin-grammar.ts` writes.

The test expects Git itself to succeed first, so a copy that is no repository fails on that rather than on a pin compared with nothing. Git can answer only from a history it holds. The gate checks the repository out whole, so the answer is the true one there. A copy with no history but its last commit names that commit for every path, and the test then fails until the history is fetched.

=>

const lastGrammarCommit: Effect.Effect<string> = Effect.sync(() => {
  const asked = spawnSync('git', ['log', '-1', '--format=%H', '--', 'src'], {
    cwd: grammarDir,
    encoding: 'utf8',
  })
  expect(asked.status).toBe(0)
  return asked.stdout.trim()
})

const pinnedRevision = (manifest: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(
      /rev = "([0-9a-f]{40})"/.exec(readFileSync(join(grammarDir, manifest), 'utf8')),
    ),
    Option.map(([, revision]) => revision),
  )

describe('the pins', () => {
  Array.forEach(['editors/zed/extension.toml', 'editors/helix/languages.toml'], (manifest) =>
    it.effect(`${manifest} pins the commit that last changed the grammar`, () =>
      Effect.map(lastGrammarCommit, (commit) =>
        expect(pinnedRevision(manifest)).toEqual(Option.some(commit)),
      ),
    ),
  )
})

## Cleaning up

The scratch folder holds the generated parser, the compiled library, and the fixtures. It goes once every test has run.

=>

afterAll(() => rmSync(scratch, { recursive: true, force: true }))

## What the test draws on

Bun's test runner supplies `describe`, `it`, `expect`, `beforeAll`, and `afterAll`, and `effectify` adds the `it.effect` that runs an Effect as a test. Effect's `Array` and `pipe` shape the lists, `Option` holds a capture a match may lack, and `Stream` collects the classifier's wefts. Node supplies the process the command runs in, the filesystem the fixtures and the scratch folder live in, and the resolver that finds the command's package. `LoomWeft` is the weft type from the AST, and `boxOf` reads the box a weft carries. `LoomSourceRanges` and `WeftClassifier` are the first two stages of Loom's parser.

=>

import { afterAll, beforeAll, describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { Array, Effect, Option, Stream, pipe } from 'effect'
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'
import { mkdtempSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { createRequire } from 'node:module'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { boxOf, type LoomWeft } from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import { LoomSourceRanges } from '@athrio/loom-lang/ast/LineRanges'
import { WeftClassifier } from '@athrio/loom-lang/ast/WeftClassifier'

const it = effectify(it_)

